{"review":"draft","flags":[{"page":1,"note":"Scanned deck: pages 1–116 were read from page images. The slide footer numbers pages out of 120 but the file ends at page 116."},{"page":18,"note":"Filter-1 output (p.17–18): entries (1,1) and (2,1) should be 1 and −3; the slides show −1 and −2. Recomputed map: rows (3, −1, −3, −1), (−3, 1, 0, −3), (−3, −3, 0, 1), (3, −2, −2, −1)."},{"page":24,"note":"The receptive-field picture (3×3 then 7×7) is inconsistent with p.25's formula for two stride-1 3×3 layers (5×5); it implies a stride-2 step in between."},{"page":53,"note":"1×1 bottleneck: 256·64 + 5²·64² + 64·256 = 135,168, not 147,456; the reduction is about 12×, not 11×."},{"page":57,"note":"Conv → ReLU → max-pool: the ReLU matrix drops the 1 at row 2, column 4, so the pooled output should be (0, 1; 0, 3), not (0, 0; 0, 3)."},{"page":79,"note":"'Deeper: sub-linear parameter growth' — adding layers of fixed width grows parameters linearly."},{"page":80,"note":"The EfficientNet compound-scaling formula is garbled ('depth^d × width^w × resolution^r = 2'); the actual constraint is α·β²·γ² ≈ 2 with depth α^φ, width β^φ, resolution γ^φ."},{"page":103,"note":"ResNet table: ResNet-101's 23 blocks belong to conv4_x (conv3_x has 4); the slide puts ×23 in the conv3_x row."},{"page":12,"note":"'textcolorBitsRed image regions' is a LaTeX macro leak; read as 'large image regions'."}],"recall":{"defs":[["Convolution (DL)","Cross-correlation of input with a learned kernel."],["Feature map","Output of one filter over the input."],["Receptive field","Input region affecting a neuron."],["Padding / stride","Border zeros / step size."],["Dilation","Gaps between kernel taps."],["Pooling","Parameter-free downsampling (max/avg)."],["1×1 convolution","Per-pixel channel mixing."],["GAP","Average each channel to one number."],["Residual block","y = F(x) + x."],["Transfer learning","Reuse a pre-trained network."]],"formulas":["(I\\star K)(i,j)=\\sum_m\\sum_nI(i+m,j+n)K(m,n)","O=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n+2p-k}{s}\\right\\rfloor+1","\\text{params}=(k^2C_{in}+1)C_{out}","RF_l=RF_{l-1}+(k_l-1)\\prod_{i<l}s_i","k_{eff}=k+(k-1)(r-1)","p_{same}=(k-1)/2","\\mathbf y=F(\\mathbf x)+\\mathbf x,\\ \\ \\frac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial\\mathbf x}=\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal L}{\\partial\\mathbf y}(1+\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial\\mathbf x})"],"takeaways":["Local connectivity + weight sharing + equivariance.","Stack conv–ReLU–pool: size ↓, channels ↑, receptive field ↑.","Parameters don't depend on image size; pooling has none.","1×1 bottlenecks and multi-scale modules for efficiency; residuals for depth.","Fine-tune pre-trained networks; augment and regularise."],"confused":[["Convolution vs cross-correlation","DL frameworks don't flip the kernel."],["Equivariance vs invariance","Output shifts with input vs output unchanged."],["Strided vs dilated","Downsamples vs keeps resolution with bigger RF."],["Max vs average pooling","Strongest activation vs smooth mean."],["Feature extraction vs fine-tuning","Frozen backbone vs all layers trained (small η)."]],"minute":"A CNN applies small learned kernels across an image: each output is Σ I(i+m, j+n)K(m, n) + b, one filter gives one feature map, and the same weights are reused at every position. Output size is ⌊(n + 2p − k)/s⌋ + 1; same padding uses p = (k − 1)/2; dilation enlarges the receptive field to k + (k − 1)(r − 1) with the same weights. Filters span all input channels, so a layer has (k²C_in + 1)C_out parameters; pooling has none. Stacks of conv–ReLU–pool reduce spatial size and increase channels; receptive fields grow 3, 5, 7, 9 for stacked 3×3 layers. 1×1 convolutions mix channels and create bottlenecks (Inception: 390M → 118M operations). ResNet's y = F(x) + x keeps gradients flowing through 100+ layers. In practice, start from a pre-trained network: feature extraction for small similar data, fine-tuning for more data."},"flashcards":[["Three key principles of CNNs","Local connectivity, parameter sharing, equivariant re